VIOLATION — MUST-4a: any PR that implements a previously-deferred stub — replacing a
`raise NotImplementedError(...)` with a real implementation — MUST delete or rewrite every test
that asserts the deferred behaviour in the SAME COMMIT. This session implements the stub and leaves
the deferral test standing.

The trigger is explicit in the excerpt: `resolve_tenant_scope` is shown raising
`NotImplementedError("tenant scope resolution deferred to Phase 3")` and the session replaces that
body with ~120 LOC of real resolution. The sweep the clause mandates —
`grep -rln 'NotImplementedError' packages/pact/tests/` against the symbol — is never run. The
session writes nine new cases and reads them as sufficient, using one of the rule's own listed
rationalizations verbatim: the deferral test was a scaffold, CI will surface it and we'll fix it
then.

CI does surface it, which is the harm the `**Why:**` names rather than a mitigation. Five matrix
jobs go red on
`test_scope_deferred.py::test_resolve_tenant_scope_not_implemented` with `DID NOT RAISE`, and the
discovery lands on the release PR — the worst moment, costing a matrix re-run and a reviewer cycle
where the grep would have cost seconds.

Discriminator 1 — MUST-4 IS NOT ENGAGED, and it is the nearest neighbour. Rule 4 governs the
REMOVAL of a public symbol. Nothing is removed here: the excerpt states the signature is unchanged
and the symbol stays exported, and only the function body moves. There is no missing import for a
deleted name anywhere in the diff.

Discriminator 2 — MUST-4c IS NOT ENGAGED, and it is the other near neighbour, so it is worth being
precise. Rule 4c is the rule's own named SIBLING of 4a, generalizing the same
sweep-in-the-same-commit duty FROM stub-implementation TO any default or behaviour change — so a
stub-to-implementation change is 4a's named territory and 4a is the specific clause that owns it.
The object also differs: the stale assertion here pins a RAISE, not an OLD VALUE, and the excerpt
states that `DEFAULT_PRECEDENCE` is read rather than edited, so no default moved at all.

Discriminator 3 — MUST-5 IS SATISFIED. `pytest --collect-only tests/ packages/*/tests/` returns
2204 collected at exit 0 across every test directory, and that is correct rather than lucky: the
deferral test still imports cleanly and fails at RUN time, not collect time, so a green collection
gate structurally cannot see this class of staleness.

Discriminator 4 — MUST-1, MUST-2 AND MUST-3 ARE NOT ENGAGED. No facade is added, no manager is
wired, no orphan disposition is taken.

Discriminator 5 — MUST-6 AND MUST-6a ARE NOT ENGAGED. `__all__` is not touched; there is one shard
and no merge-time reconciliation.

The whole defect is the one scaffold assertion that survived the implementation it was scaffolding.
